    "advanced": {
      "title": "Adverbs of Manner — -ment Formation, Irregular Forms & Position",
      "explanation": "Adverbs of manner in French are primarily formed by attaching '-ment' to the feminine singular form of an adjective. Key rules: (1) Adjectives ending in a consonant in the masculine take the feminine form: 'lent' → 'lente' → 'lentement.' (2) Adjectives already ending in a vowel add '-ment' directly: 'rapide' → 'rapidement,' 'facile' → 'facilement,' 'vrai' → 'vraiment.' (3) Adjectives ending in -ent/-ant change to -emment/-amment: 'patient' → 'patiemment,' 'constant' → 'constamment,' 'prudent' → 'prudemment,' 'diligent' → 'diligemment.' (4) Irregular adverbs exist: 'bon' → 'bien' (well), 'mauvais' → 'mal' (badly), 'meilleur' → 'mieux' (better). Position: Adverbs of manner typically follow the verb, often positioned after the direct object if one exists. Example: 'Elle chante la chanson magnifiquement' (She sings the song magnificently).",
      "examples": [
        {
          "french": "Il parle lentement.",
          "english": "He speaks slowly."
        },
        {
          "french": "Elle marche doucement dans le parc.",
          "english": "She walks softly in the park."
        },
        {
          "french": "Ils travaillent patiemment.",
          "english": "They work patiently."
        },
        {
          "french": "Elle parle facilement plusieurs langues.",
          "english": "She speaks several languages easily."
        },
        {
          "french": "Il joue du piano magnifiquement.",
          "english": "He plays piano magnificently."
        },
        {
          "french": "Ils chantent joyeusement.",
          "english": "They sing joyfully."
        },
        {
          "french": "Elle répond intelligemment à la question.",
          "english": "She answers the question intelligently."
        },
        {
          "french": "Il conduit prudemment.",
          "english": "He drives carefully."
        }
      ],
